WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:03.730
- [Man] One and lift off!

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:08.060
The rise of Starliner and a
new era in human space flight.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.400
- [Presenter] In 2019,
Boeing and NASA attempted

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.380
to send an uncrewed spacecraft

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.100
to the International Space Station

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.240
but the test flight failed
to reach the correct orbit.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.800
It was just one in a series of issues

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.430
the company's space program
has faced in recent years.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.610
Now, as Boeing gears up
to launch Starliner again,

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.390
all eyes are on the company.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:31.100
If it succeeds the test
flight could renew investor

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.360
and customer confidence
in Boeing space program.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.400
But if it fails, it could put a dent

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.030
in the already weakened aerospace company.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.450
- So it's a big challenge
with very high stakes

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:46.380
and a lot of national and
corporate pride at stake

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.000
and a lot of money at stake.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:48.833
- [Presenter] Here's how Boeing,

00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:51.160
which was once the gold
standard of the stars,

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:52.333
got to this point.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.270
For decades Boeing was at the cutting edge

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:57.770
of the space industry.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:01.200
- They've been involved in
every major NASA development.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.600
- [Presenter] This is Steven Ganote.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.860
A space industry veteran who's consulted

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.950
in the industry for 15 years.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:10.450
- They built critical
communication satellites for NASA

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.340
and for others in the commercial

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.590
and the national security industry.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.950
- [Presenter] In 2014 Boeing
was awarded a NASA contract

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.820
worth as much as $4.2 billion

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.680
but just four years later
an external audit found

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.070
that the world's largest
aerospace company was struggling.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.560
At October, 2018, at the
direction of Congress,

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.620
NASA released an audit of
its Space Launch System,

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.460
also known as the SLS.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.350
This Deep Space Explorer
is the most powerful rocket

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:40.410
NASA has ever developed and is one

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:43.130
of the agency's largest
contracts with Boeing.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:46.640
NASA audit found that the
project was years behind schedule

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.180
and billions of dollars
over budget due in part

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.060
to management, technical and
infrastructure issues driven

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.040
by Boeing's poor performance.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.920
- What the auditor's
report did was surface

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.290
to the space division some
of the engineering challenges

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.620
that seem to have seeped
throughout the company,

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:08.070
both its broader defense and
commercial jetliner business.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.830
- [Presenter] Doug
Cameron has covered Boeing

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:12.140
and the larger aerospace
and defense industry

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.800
for over 30 years.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.730
He explains that as the development

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.200
of the Space Launch System continued,

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.780
Boeing was juggling another
high profile contract

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.110
with NASA as well.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.190
The Commercial Crew Program
which aims to send astronauts

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:28.260
and cargo to and from the
international space station

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.820
inside of Boeing Starliner crew capsule.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:35.210
- Boeing was the original
prime contractor and integrator

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:38.140
for the International Space
Station so they helped design

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.060
and implement that from inception

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.430
and they still helped to
operate the space station.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:45.880
And being able to bring
astronauts up on a Boeing capsule

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.750
to the station is something
that Boeing has set out

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.380
as a major goal for its space program.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.920
(rocket roaring)

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.120
- [Presenter] When Starliner
launched in December, 2019,

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.210
it was a critical moment for Boeing.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.420
But just minutes into the test flight,

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:04.660
NASA's controllers knew
something had gone wrong.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:07.900
- So we did have, obviously,
some challenges today.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:12.270
When the spacecraft separated
from the launch vehicle,

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.830
we did not get the orbital insertion burn

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:16.380
that we were hoping for.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.660
- [Presenter] The spacecraft's
internal timer was off

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.720
by 11 hours stranding
it in the wrong orbit

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.410
which prevented it from being able

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:26.020
to reach the International Space Station.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:29.430
- The failure in 2019 of the
On-Orbit Test to Starliner

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:33.790
was driven primarily
due to faulty software

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:37.710
and maybe more importantly
faulty software testing.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:41.840
Boeing chose to design tests in a way

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.020
that were not truly representative

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:47.460
or tied to the actual vehicle itself.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.240
- [Presenter] And these
mistakes were costly.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.080
Boeing has taken a $410 million charge

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.000
primarily to run a second test.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.490
This is about 10% of the
program's entire budget.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:00.380
A Boeing spokesperson said that
since the first test flight

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.310
the company has made important
changes to the software code

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:05.150
that are consistent
with the recommendations

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:08.270
of the Boeing-NASA
independent review team.

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:13.270
- So I think Boeing has invested
in this both with its brand

00:04:13.275 --> 00:04:17.390
as a great and historic
engineering company

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:20.550
that has been serving
NASA since its inception

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:24.680
but also financially because
Boeing, as we all know,

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.930
overall is in much more
difficult financial circumstances

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.540
due to the pandemic and problems

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.240
with their commercial aircraft

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.100
and that puts an even higher spotlight

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.640
on the performance of Starliner.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:40.190
- [Presenter] Since Starliner's
orbital failure in 2019,

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:42.590
Boeing space program has faced a series

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.280
of additional challenges.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.640
In 2020, federal prosecutors
opened a criminal probe

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.350
into whether a senior NASA
official improperly told

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.190
a Boeing executive about the status

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.630
of a lunar lander contract
causing the company

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:56.170
to change its bid.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:59.260
Boeing ultimately lost the
contract to competitors

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:03.040
and in January, 2021 the
company faced another setback

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.060
when the main engines

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.950
for the Space Launch System
shut down prematurely

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:08.270
during a ground test.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:09.970
- These problems are to be expected

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:11.460
that's why you do the testing.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.580
The problem for Boeing is
it's the latest in a series

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.280
of very high profile failures
and obviously have customers

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:21.200
and Boeing investors asking the question

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.010
when will they get it right?

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:24.570
- [Presenter] But despite years of issues

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:26.570
and wavering faith from NASA,

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:30.230
Boeing has seen successes in
the space industry as well.

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:33.600
It's a 50:50 partner in
the United Launch Alliance,

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.720
a company which has successfully launched

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.940
over 140 consecutive missions,

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:40.860
more than any other launch
program in the world.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.660
Boeing has also developed
satellites, spacesuits

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.680
and other technology for NASA

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:48.210
and continues to play a
key role in the maintenance

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:50.420
of the International Space Station.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:53.830
For Boeing, the space program
is a key part of the company.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:56.800
It's part of its Defense,
Space and Security unit,

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.260
Boeing's largest by
revenue after a sharp drop

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:02.070
in aircraft deliveries
over the past two years.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:05.000
With travel demand still
weak due to the pandemic,

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:09.490
experts say Boeing will likely
continue to rely on the unit.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.330
This is one reason all eyes are

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:14.030
on the company's next
test launch of Starliner.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:17.500
The spacecraft's second uncrewed
flight was slated for March

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:18.710
but has been pushed back

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:20.970
due to scheduling and repair delays.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:22.730
For Boeing, the pressure is on.

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:25.740
The company is already far
behind competitor SpaceX

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:29.040
which has successfully
launched astronauts to the ISS

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.640
as part of NASA's Commercial Crew Program.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:33.740
- The entire industry
will be looking at Boeing

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:36.210
who's already named astronauts
that it would like to fly.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.520
Those astronauts are
ready and waiting to go up

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:41.330
on a Boeing vehicle but Boeing needs

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.690
to make sure that vehicle is safe.

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:46.450
It needs to make sure that
not only all of the mechanical

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:49.440
and normal challenges are
addressed but all of the software

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:52.170
and the software testing
and oversight challenges

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:55.160
have been addressed and have
been managed by the company.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:56.870
- [Presenter] If the launch is a success,

00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:58.140
astronauts could be flying

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.840
to the International Space Station

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.660
inside Boeing Starliner later this year.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:05.290
This would be a big win
for Boeing at large.

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.400
The company CEO, David Calhoun,

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:09.630
said that Boeing was,
"Proud of all the products

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:11.710
and services our engineers have developed

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:14.410
and delivered to our commercial
and military customers

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:16.020
over these last difficult years

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:17.950
and of the meaningful
progress we are making

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.920
in safety, transparency and quality."

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:22.330
The success or failure

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:25.270
of Starliner will help
determine the future of Boeing

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:26.900
and its role in the exploration

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:29.033
and commercialization of outer space.

